  "verdict": "divergent",
  "divergences": [
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "description": "Lean requires K to contain one consistency sentence for F’s Craig presentation. The source’s antecedent is knowledge of F’s soundness, and its stated weaker alternative is knowledge of every local reflection instance, not just consistency. The remark on p. 356 makes determining consistency a necessary part of determining correctness; it does not offer knowledge of consistency as a substitute antecedent."
    },
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "description": "Lean assumes `F.theory ⊆ K`. In the source, this containment is part of the conclusion to be obtained from known correctness. Once containment is assumed, Lean need only establish that K has a sentence outside `F.theory`."
    },
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "description": "Lean restricts F to recursively enumerable theories proving every theorem of IΣ₁, and takes the actual consistency of F as a separate premise. Even if effective presentation is implicit in “formal system” and consistency follows from genuinely known soundness, the source does not impose the IΣ₁ lower bound on F. The EA lower bound in the quoted theorem is on S, not F."
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "The advertised membership proxy represents acceptance externally: K is an arbitrary set of arithmetic sentences, with no factivity or closure requirement. It supplies neither an object-language operator for absolute provability nor a way to apply that operator to the global soundness assertion `F ⊆ T`."
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The strict-inclusion conclusion matches, but the route to it does not. Lean states that an already included, sufficiently strong consistent theory is strictly exceeded by a set containing its consistency sentence, rather than deriving strict inclusion from known correctness."
